        <div id="what-is-14-50" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The NEMA 14-50R, Explained
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Four Prongs, Two Voltages</h3>

          <p>
            A 14-50R is a four-prong, 120/240V, 50 amp receptacle. The four slots are two hot legs, a neutral and a ground. Between either hot and neutral you get 120V; between the two hots you get 240V. It is the same receptacle used for electric ranges, many EV chargers and the 50 amp pedestal at an RV park, which is why a generator with one can plug into so many different things.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Rating Is the Outlet, Not the Output</h3>

          <p>
            The 50 in the name is the rating of the receptacle and the circuit behind it, not a measure of what the generator produces. A 14-50R on a generator means the outlet and its breaker are built to handle up to 50 amps on each hot leg. What actually flows is set by the alternator and engine. That distinction is the single most important thing to understand before you buy a 50 amp generator, and the next section does the math.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Related Outlets and Other Names</h3>

          <p>
            You will also see a few close relatives on generator panels. The L14-30R is a four-prong twist-lock rated 30 amps at 120/240V, the outlet most home transfer switches and interlock inlets are built around. The TT-30R is the 120V-only, 30 amp RV outlet on smaller inverters. The 14-30R is the dryer receptacle in your laundry room, a different and smaller outlet. None of these is interchangeable with a 14-50R without an adapter, and some of the adapters sold for them are a bad idea.
          </p>

          <p>
            Generator manufacturers sometimes call the 14-50R a 50A heavy duty outlet or an RV ready 50 amp outlet. DuroMax's spec sheets, for example, list a 120/240V 50A heavy duty outlet. It is the same receptacle; check the spec sheet for the NEMA number or the four-prong 120/240V description to be sure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="amp-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why a 50 Amp Outlet Does Not Mean 12,000 Watts
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The 12,000 Watt Math</h3>

          <p>
            Here is the math that most listings skip. A 50 amp circuit at 240V can carry 50 x 240 = 12,000 watts. An RV's 50 amp service is two 120V legs of 50 amps each, which is the same 12,000 watts. So a generator would need about 12,000 running watts to fill a 14-50R completely. Very few portables come close, and none of the 9,000 to 9,500 watt generators that commonly carry this outlet do.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Divide Running Watts by 240</h3>

          <p>
            To find what a generator can actually supply through its 14-50R, divide its running watts by 240. That gives the running amps per leg at 240V, which is also the most each 120V leg can carry when the load is balanced. Manufacturers often print it on the spec sheet.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Generator</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Running Watts (Gas)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Amps at 240V</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Share of 50A</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
