                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">37.5A</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">75%</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Per-Leg Limit</h3>

          <p>
            The per-leg limit matters most for RVs and for unbalanced home loads. A 9,500 watt generator can supply about 4,750 watts on each 120V leg, not 9,500 watts on one. If all of your big loads sit on one leg of the RV panel, that leg hits its limit and the generator's breaker trips while the other leg is nearly idle. Balancing loads across both legs is how you get the full rating.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Starting Watts Do Not Change It</h3>

          <p>
            Starting watts do not change this picture much. Peak ratings tell you how much surge the generator can deliver for a moment while a compressor starts; they do not raise the continuous current. Size the plan on running watts, and use our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a> for the appliance numbers.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Running watts divided by 240 is the real amp rating.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A 14-50R tells you what plug fits. The running watts tell you what you can use. Below about 12,000 running watts, you are buying a 50 amp connector on a 35 to 48 amp generator.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="which-have-one" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Portables Actually Have a 14-50R
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Roughly 9,000 Running Watts and Up</h3>

          <p>
            In practice, a true 120/240V 14-50R shows up on portables rated at roughly 9,000 running watts and above. Below that, manufacturers fit an L14-30R for home use and a TT-30R for RVs, because a 30 amp outlet already covers everything a 7,500 watt generator can make: 30 x 240 = 7,200 watts. Putting a 50 amp receptacle on a smaller generator would mostly invite overloading.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Most 14-50R Models Live</h3>

          <p>
            The dual fuel open-frame segment is where most 14-50R models live: Westinghouse's WGen9500DF and WGen11500DFc, DuroMax's XP12000EH and XP13000EH, and similar units from other brands. Large inverters are starting to add the outlet too, as the Champion 201417 does, and those tend to rate it below 50 amps on the spec sheet. Read the outlet's amp rating, not just its shape.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Parallel Kits and 50 Amp RV Receptacles</h3>

          <p>
            Some smaller inverters advertise 50 amp RV capability through a parallel kit. When two inverters are linked, some kits add a 50 amp RV receptacle that is fed at 120V on both hot legs. That works for many RVs, which mostly use 120V appliances, but it is not 240V, so it cannot feed a 240V well pump or a home transfer switch that expects split-phase power. Check the kit's documentation before counting on it for anything but an RV.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Shopping by Size</h3>

          <p>
            If you are shopping by size rather than by outlet, the <a href="/best-10000-watt-generator/">best 10000 watt generators</a> guide covers the class where 14-50R outlets are most common, and the <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generators</a> pillar covers the whole range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rv-hookup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Running a 50 Amp RV on a Generator
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How the Coach Splits the Load</h3>

          <p>
            A 50 amp RV has a 14-50P plug on its shore power cord, which fits a generator's 14-50R directly with no adapter. Inside the coach, the panel is split into two 120V legs, and the big loads, usually two roof air conditioners, the microwave, the water heater element and the residential fridge if fitted, are divided between them. The generator's job is to keep both legs inside its per-leg limit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Budgeting Each Leg</h3>

          <p>
            With a 9,500 watt generator, plan on roughly 4,750 watts per leg. A typical approach is one air conditioner per leg with the water heater on propane, and the microwave run only when the second air conditioner is cycled off. With an 11,500 watt generator, you have about 5,750 watts per leg, which gives real margin for both air conditioners plus cooking. Many coaches include an energy management system that sheds loads automatically when current approaches a limit; set it for the generator's amperage, not the coach's 50 amp rating, if it allows that.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Snag One: The Neutral-Ground Bond</h3>

          <p>
            Two practical snags come up often. First, many RV surge protectors and energy management systems check for a neutral-to-ground bond and report an open ground on a generator with a floating neutral. If that happens, the usual fix for RV use is a neutral-ground bonding plug in a spare 120V outlet. Never use a bonding plug when the generator feeds a house through a transfer switch that does not switch the neutral, because the house already bonds neutral and ground at the main panel.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Snag Two: The Dogbone Adapter</h3>

          <p>
            Second, a 30 to 50 amp dogbone adapter lets a 50 amp RV plug into a TT-30R, but it feeds both legs from a single 30 amp, 120V source. You get 3,600 watts total, not 50 amps. It is fine for running lights and a fridge from a small inverter; it is not a 50 amp hookup. For generators built around RV use more broadly, including 30 amp setups, see our <a href="/best-rv-generator/">best RV generators</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-hookup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Using a 14-50R for Home Backup
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Inlet Box and Transfer Equipment</h3>

          <p>
            For a house, the 14-50R connects to a 50 amp power inlet box mounted on the outside wall, which feeds either a manual transfer switch or an interlocked backfeed breaker in the main panel. Many 50 amp generator inlets use the California-style CS6375 receptacle, so the cord typically runs from a 14-50P at the generator to a matching CS6364 connector at the house. Confirm the inlet type before you buy the cord.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Transfer Switch or Interlock</h3>

          <p>
            The choice between a transfer switch and an interlock is the same as at 30 amps. A transfer switch moves selected circuits to generator power; an interlock kit mechanically prevents the main breaker and the generator breaker from being on at the same time, letting you power any circuit in the panel. Our comparison of an <a href="/interlock-kit-vs-transfer-switch/">interlock kit vs transfer switch</a> covers the trade-offs, and the <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">best manual transfer switch</a> guide includes 50 amp options.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Generator Is Still the Limit</h3>

          <p>
            A 50 amp inlet and breaker do not make the generator a 50 amp source. The breaker protects the wiring; the generator's own breaker protects the generator. A 9,500 watt unit will still trip at around 40 amps per leg, so a 50 amp inlet on that generator mainly buys you a cord and connector with more headroom and an upgrade path. If your generator is under about 9,000 watts, a 30 amp inlet and an L14-30 cord are cheaper and fully adequate.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hire a Licensed Electrician</h3>

          <p>
            This is licensed electrician territory in most places. The inlet, the interlock or switch, the breaker and the wire size all have code requirements, and a permit is often required. Our guide to <a href="/generator-transfer-switch-types-cost-and-installation/">transfer switch types, cost and installation</a> explains what is involved and what it usually costs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cord-gauge" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cord Gauge: 6/4 vs 6/3 With Ground
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Four Conductors, 6 AWG</h3>

          <p>
            A 14-50 cord needs four conductors: two hots, a neutral and a ground. For a 50 amp circuit, the hots and neutral should be 6 AWG copper. You will see these cords labeled two ways. A 6/4 cord has four 6 AWG conductors, one of which is the ground. A cord labeled 6/3 with an 8 AWG ground, sometimes written 6/3 + 8/1, has three 6 AWG conductors plus a smaller ground. Both are common and legitimate for 50 amp generator and RV cords; the difference is mostly the ground size and the price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never 10 AWG on a 14-50 Plug</h3>

          <p>
            What is not acceptable is 10 AWG wire on a 14-50 plug. A 10/4 cord is a 30 amp cord, and cheap adapters that put a 50 amp plug on 30 amp wire can overheat when a large generator pushes 40 amps through them. Check the jacket printing: it should say 6, not 8 or 10, for the main conductors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Length and Voltage Drop</h3>

          <p>
            Length matters too. Voltage drop rises with length, and at 40 amps a long run can drop enough voltage to make motors and compressors work harder. Most 50 amp generator cords are sold in 25 and 30 foot lengths, which suits the minimum safe distance from the house. If your run is much longer, size up or relocate the inlet; our <a href="/generator-extension-cord-size/">generator extension cord size</a> guide shows the voltage drop math.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Jacket, Ends and Handling</h3>

          <p>
            Look for a cord jacket rated for outdoor use, such as STW or SOOW, molded or properly clamped ends, and a locking or weather cover at the inlet. A 6 AWG cord is heavy and stiff, so uncoil it fully when in use; a coiled cord carrying high current can heat up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="50-vs-30" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. 14-50R vs L14-30R: When 30 Amps Is Enough
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What an L14-30R Covers</h3>

          <p>
            Every generator in this guide also has an L14-30R, and for many buyers that is the outlet they will actually use. An L14-30 circuit carries up to 30 x 240 = 7,200 watts. If your outage plan is a well pump, refrigerator, freezer, furnace blower and lights, it probably fits under that, and a 30 amp inlet, cord and transfer switch cost less than the 50 amp versions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Three Cases for the 14-50R</h3>

          <p>
            The 14-50R earns its place in three cases. The first is a 50 amp RV, where the 14-50P on the coach plugs straight in. The second is a home plan above about 7,200 watts that includes central air, an electric water heater or an electric range; at that point the L14-30 becomes the limit, and the 14-50R lets a 9,500 to 11,500 watt generator deliver its full output. The third is simply future-proofing: if you plan to move up to a larger generator, a 50 amp inlet installed now avoids doing the job twice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Add Up Your Running Watts</h3>

          <p>
            If you are unsure, add up the running watts you want at once. Under 7,000 watts, the L14-30R is enough and the 50 amp outlet is a nice extra. Over 7,500 watts, use the 14-50R, or you are paying for a generator you cannot fully connect. For central air sizing specifically, see <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-central-air-conditioning/">what size generator runs central air conditioning</a>.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Never Backfeed</h3>

          <p>
            Never run two separate cords from the L14-30R and the 14-50R into the house at the same time, and never use a male-to-male cord into a range or dryer receptacle. Backfeeding through an appliance outlet bypasses every safety device in the panel and can energize the utility line.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="setup-safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Setup and Safety at 50 Amps
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outdoors Only, 20 Feet Away</h3>

          <p>
            A generator big enough to fill a 14-50R burns a lot of fuel and makes a lot of carbon monoxide. Run it outdoors only, at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents. Our guide on <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> goes through distances and weather protection. The Westinghouse WGen11500DFc and Champion 201417 add CO shutoffs; for the others, battery-powered CO alarms inside are not optional.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plug In, Then Bring Loads Up</h3>

          <p>
            Plug in with the generator running and its breakers off, or with the RV's main breaker off, then switch on and bring loads up one at a time, largest motor first. Starting two air conditioners at once on a 9,500 watt generator is the most common cause of nuisance trips. On a house, turn on the well pump or air conditioner first and let it settle before adding smaller loads.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Grounding</h3>

          <p>
            Grounding depends on how the generator is connected. When it feeds a house through a transfer switch or interlock, the house's grounding system usually serves; when it stands alone, check the manual. Our explainer on <a href="/do-i-need-to-ground-a-portable-generator/">whether you need to ground a portable generator</a> covers the cases.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Inspect the Plug Every Season</h3>

          <p>
            Finally, inspect the 14-50 plug and receptacle every season. A loose or burned blade at 40 amps generates real heat. Discoloration, a melted face or a plug that falls out easily means the part needs replacing before the next outage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size generator do I need for a 50 amp RV?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                To use most of a 50 amp RV service you need about 9,000 running watts or more, which gives roughly 37 to 40 amps per leg. Two roof air conditioners plus normal loads usually fit on a 9,500 watt generator if you balance the legs. An 11,500 watt generator gives real margin for cooking and both air conditioners together.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a generator with a 50 amp outlet run my whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the house. A 9,500 to 11,500 watt generator through a 50 amp inlet can run a well pump, refrigerator, freezer, furnace, lights and often a central air unit, but not an electric range, water heater and dryer at once. Add up running watts and stay under the generator's rating.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 14-50R the same as an RV 50 amp outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. RV parks use the NEMA 14-50R for 50 amp service, and 50 amp RVs have a 14-50P plug. A generator's 14-50R accepts that plug directly. The difference is how much current is behind it: a park pedestal supplies 50 amps per leg, while most portables supply 35 to 48 amps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What gauge cord do I need for a 50 amp generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use a 6 AWG cord with four conductors, labeled 6/4 or 6/3 with an 8 AWG ground. Keep it as short as practical, typically 25 to 30 feet, and choose an outdoor-rated jacket such as STW or SOOW. Never use 10 AWG wire with a 50 amp plug.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug a 50 amp RV into a 30 amp generator outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with a dogbone adapter into a TT-30R, but the RV then gets a single 30 amp, 120V feed on both legs, or about 3,600 watts. That runs lights, a fridge and maybe one air conditioner, not a 50 amp coach's full load. An L14-30R to 14-50 adapter is a different case and needs care with load.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a 50 amp transfer switch for a 50 amp generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you want to use the generator's full output in the house. A generator over about 7,500 running watts can exceed what a 30 amp switch and L14-30 cord carry, so a 50 amp inlet and switch or interlock make sense. Below that, a 30 amp setup is cheaper and adequate.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my RV surge protector say open ground on the generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Many portables have a floating neutral, meaning neutral and ground are not bonded at the generator. RV surge protectors look for that bond and flag it as a fault. A neutral-ground bonding plug in a spare outlet is the usual fix for RV use, but remove it if the generator feeds a house.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the 14-50R delivers 50 amps</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 14-50R is a connector rating. Divide the generator's running watts by 240 to get the amps it can supply. Most 9,500 watt portables manage about 40 amps per leg; plan loads around that number, not the 50 on the outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Loading one leg of an RV panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each 120V leg of a 50 amp RV can only draw half the generator's output. Put one air conditioner on each leg, run the water heater on propane, and avoid stacking the microwave on the same leg as an air conditioner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a 10-gauge cord with a 50 amp plug</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 50 amp cord needs 6 AWG hots and neutral, labeled 6/4 or 6/3 with an 8 AWG ground. A 10/4 cord is rated for 30 amps and can overheat when a large generator pushes 40 amps through it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding through a range or dryer outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A male-to-male cord into a 14-50R range receptacle or a dryer outlet bypasses the panel's protection and can energize utility lines. Use a 50 amp inlet with an interlock or transfer switch, installed to code.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving a bonding plug in for home use</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A neutral-ground bonding plug can fix an open-ground warning on an RV surge protector, but leave it out when the generator feeds a house through a transfer switch or interlock, since the house panel already bonds neutral and ground.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

